;; std/editor/serve.blsp — serve a `ui-run` app to remote frontends.
;;
;; The Emacs `--daemon` / `emacsclient` model (M4, ADR-090): the **app runs on the
;; daemon** — its model, `view`, and `update` all live in the serving runtime — and a
;; **thin client** just paints the frames the daemon pushes and ships back the keys it
;; reads. "The frontend is a protocol": the same render frame (plain Brood data) and the
;; same key values cross a node link instead of going to a local terminal.
;;
;; This is the *push* direction, the complement of the observer's remote-attach (which
;; pulls snapshots and runs the loop on the client). Here the daemon runs the unmodified
;; `(ui-run model view update display)` — the trick is the **display**: `remote-display`
;; is a frontend whose `:draw` sends the frame to the client and whose `:poll` receives
;; the client's keys. So an app written for a local terminal serves to a remote one with
;; no change (ADR-046: one display protocol, many frontends — now a *network* frontend).
;;
;;     ;; daemon — `nest run --name ed app.blsp`, whose main registers the app and
;;     ;; then blocks, so the node stays alive after main would otherwise return:
;;     (defn main ()
;;       (serve (fn () {:n 0}) my-view my-update)
;;       (receive (_ (main))))     ; park: any park loop works; `serve` itself doesn't block
;;     ;; client (another terminal / machine):
;;     (attach "ed")              ; or "ed@host:port"; like `emacsclient -s ed`
;;
;; **Many clients** can attach at once — each gets its own session process running its
;; own `ui-run` over a fresh `(make-model)`, so sessions are independent. A *shared*
;; model across clients (collaborative editing) and per-client viewports onto shared
;; buffers are deferred (ADR-011); a client's resize after attach is live (ADR-587) — the slice proves
;; the seam. Detach, client death, and a dropped link all tear the session down cleanly.
;; A session SUPERVISES its loop (ADR-593): the loop dying runs the app's crash hook and
;; restarts it, within a budget, for the same client — see `serve`'s `:supervise`.

(defmodule editor/serve
  "Serve a `ui-run` app to remote frontends — the Emacs `--daemon`/`emacsclient`
model (ADR-090). The app's model/`view`/`update` run on the daemon; a thin client
(`editor/attach`, or `nest attach NAME`) paints pushed frames and ships back keys. `serve`
registers the app under the well-known node name `serve-name` (`:ui`, `editor/serve-protocol`); each attaching
client gets its own session process running `ui-run` against a `remote-display` —
the frontend whose `:draw` sends the frame over the node link and whose `:poll`
receives the client's input. Many clients may attach at once
(independent sessions). Each session supervises its loop: one that dies is restarted for
the same client from what it retained (`serve-retain`), after the app's crash hook
(`:supervise`, ADR-593). Built purely on `ui-run` + the node-link primitives."
  (:use editor/ui)
  (:use editor/frontend)
  (:use editor/serve-protocol))

(defdyn editor/serve/*served-client* nil)
;; In a served session's process (`serve`), a 0-arg fn answering the client that session
;; draws to now — nil while it is parked; nil outside a served session, and in a
;; `serve-shared` one, which has no one client (ADR-539).

(defdyn editor/serve/*served-client-can* nil)
;; frames (`:can` in its attach identity — `:primary`, ADR-562 — which the session and the
;; manager note as `[:client-can client capabilities]`); `#{}` for a client that said nothing,
;; which is every client older than the saying.
;; for a client that said nothing, which is every client older than the saying.

(defdyn editor/serve/*served-session* nil)
;; In a served session's loop process (`serve`), the SESSION's pid — its supervisor, which
;; outlives the loop (ADR-593): what a loop hands what should survive it ([[serve-retain]]),
;; and what owns state that lives as long as the session (a buffer registry's `:owner`).
;; nil outside one.

;; ---- the seam: a display frontend that lives on the wire ---------------------

; nil ⇒ ui-run treats it as :tick (re-render)
(defn remote-display (client cols rows &optional resume supervision)
  "A `ui-run` display (`{:enter :leave :size :draw :poll}`) bound to a remote
frontend `client` (its pid): `:draw` ships the frame to `client` as `[:frame f]`,
`:poll` returns the next key the client shipped back (`[:key k held?]`, `held?`
whether a key is down in the client's window, which `:key-held?` then answers, so the
session's `ui-run` holds its idle timers through a held key as a local window's does; a
client that sends `[:key k]` is taken as holding none). Runs inside the
session process (the one `ui-run` runs in), so the client's keys land in *this*
process's mailbox. `:size` is the `[cols rows]` the client reported at attach, and then the
size of each `[:resize cols rows]` it ships (ADR-587). `:leave` tells the client to restore its
terminal (`[:bye]`). A `[:detach]` from the client returns `:close` — which `ui-run`
treats as an immediate quit, tearing the session down.

The client's death — it crashed, or its link dropped — is `:close` too, unless the session
RESUMES (`resume`, `{:ms :manager :token :monitor}` from a `serve` given `:resume-ms`; ADR-519).
Then the session PARKS instead: it keeps running with nobody to draw to, tells `:manager`
so (`[:parked session token]`), and the app folds `[:client-lost]`, its cue to make what
the client left behind safe. A manager that hands it a new client (`[:adopt token client
cols rows]`) un-parks it: the new client is told the session (`[:attached session]`) and
given the window as it stands — its options, the textures and sound data still live, the
last frame without the sounds it already played — the size becomes the new client's, and
the app folds `[:client-back]`. A manager may also ask a session that still HAS a client
to hand itself over (`[:claim token client cols rows]`), when its user attached again
before the old link's death was known: see [[remote-claim]]. A session still parked `:ms`
after it parked ends (exit reason `:client-gone`); `:forever` waits for an adopter.

Any OTHER mailbox message is returned as app input, matching the local displays
(`gui-display`/`editor/frontend/*term-display*` poll the whole mailbox): a served app's async event
bus — spawned-task replies, buffer-process pushes (`[:buffer-updated …]`), redirected
output — reaches its `update` exactly as it would locally. Without the pass-through
those messages would strand in the session's mailbox (a selective receive never
consumes them), leaving a served app deaf to everything but keys.

`supervision`, when given, is `{:session :ledger :restarted? :parked-at}` (ADR-593).
`:session` is the session's SUPERVISOR, which outlives this loop: the display tells it who the
client is, its size and when the session parked whenever one changes
(`[:editor/serve/session-state …]`), so a loop restarted after this one died serves the same
client at the same size — or, parked, lives only as long as the session had left — and is
told which of the manager's hand-overs this loop HANDLED (`[:editor/serve/handled message]`),
so a restarted loop is given again only the ones no loop did (ADR-605). It tells an
adopting client the supervisor (`[:session session]`, after `[:attached …]`) — the pid whose
end, not this loop's, ends the client. `:ledger` is a table the session owns, where the display notes the
sequence number of each numbered key it TAKES (`[:key k held? seq]`, as a client attached to
a supervised session ships them): a restarted loop tells the client the last one, and the
client sends again every key after it — the keys the dead loop never took; a new client
(an adopter, a claimant) numbers its keys from 1, so the ledger is cleared when one takes the
session over (ADR-605). `:restarted?`
holds the client's input back until the loop has folded the events queued ahead of it (its
identity, `[:session-restarted …]`; `[:editor/serve/started]` marks their end), so a key
typed through the restart is never folded before them. A `client` of nil with `resume` is a
restarted loop of a PARKED session: it starts parked, at the session's `:parked-at` — the time
its client was lost, not the restart, so a loop that keeps dying cannot keep a parked session
alive past its time to live.

    (editor/serve/remote-display client 80 24)   ;~> {:enter … :leave … :size … :draw … :poll …} shipping frames to `client`"
  ;; the display's state, in a table the session process owns: the client it serves now
  ;; (nil while parked) and the monitor on it, the size, whether a key is down there, the
  ;; quit key (to re-arm for an adopter), when it parked — and, for a resuming session, the
  ;; window a new client is given: the last frame, the options, the live textures and sounds
  (let (state (table/new))
    (do
      (table/put state :client client)
      (table/put state :monitor (:monitor resume))
      (table/put state :size [cols rows])
      (table/put state :session (:session supervision))
      (table/put state :ledger (:ledger supervision))
      (table/put state :awaiting-start (= true (:restarted? supervision)))
      ;; a restarted loop of a parked session: parked from its start, since the session parked
      (when (and (nil? client) resume)
        (table/put state :parked-at (or (:parked-at supervision) (os/now))))
      (merge
        {:enter (fn ()
                  (serve-busy-start state
                    (fn (on) (serve-to state (fn (to) (serve-send-busy to on))))))
         ; the *client* owns the real terminal, not us
         :leave (fn () (serve-to state (fn (to) (send to [:bye]))))
         :size (fn () (table/get state :size))
         ;; whether a key is down in the CLIENT's window, as its last key said: every change
         ;; to it comes with a key, so this is as current as the input the loop has taken.
         ;; The table dies with the session process, which made it
         :key-held? (fn () (table/get state :held? false))
         ;; newest-wins at the source (ADR-492): a frame still queued for a client that has
         ;; not taken it is replaced by the next, here or in the client node's mailbox. A
         ;; resuming session keeps it, for the client that adopts it
         :draw (fn (f)
                 (do
                   (when resume (table/put state :frame f))
                   (serve-to state (fn (to) (serve-send-frame to f)))))
         ;; The pixel-space seam (ADR-374) over the wire: a texture, its release, and the
         ;; window options an app asks for after attaching (`:input :pixels`, a title) each
         ;; go to the client as a message its loop applies to its own display — so a game
         ;; written against a display map serves to a thin client's window unchanged. A
         ;; resuming session remembers what is live, for a client that adopts it.
         :texture (fn (tex rgba w h)
                    (serve-seam state resume :textures tex [:texture tex rgba w h]))
         :texture-free (fn (tex) (serve-seam state resume :textures tex [:texture-free tex]))
         :sound (fn (snd pcm rate channels)
                  (serve-seam state resume :sounds snd [:sound-data snd pcm rate channels]))
         :sound-free (fn (snd) (serve-seam state resume :sounds snd [:sound-free snd]))
         :configure (fn (o)
                      (do
                        (when resume
                          (table/put state :window (merge (table/get state :window {}) o)))
                        (serve-to state (fn (to) (send to [:window o])))))
         ;; The client's input is what it ships: its keys, its detach, its death. Taken
         ;; ahead of the session's background messages (ADR-490) — every clause is
         ;; tag-led, so the scan passes a backlog without rebuilding any of it.
         :poll-input (fn ()
                       (if (table/get state :awaiting-start false)
                         nil
                         (serve-busy-kick state (remote-take-input state resume))))
         ;; the client ships a key as `[:key k held?]` over the link, where no lane survives:
         ;; the quit key is that shape (ADR-491), so a C-g interrupts a busy session. Kept,
         ;; so an adopting client's C-g is armed too
         :quit-message (fn (key) (do (table/put state :quit-key key) [:key key]))
         ;; and only from this client: a guest link that can reach the session must not stop it
         :quit-from client
         ;; the client it serves NOW — nil while parked — for what the session asks of the
         ;; client's machine (its clipboard: [[client-clipboard-set]], ADR-539)
         :served-client (fn () (table/get state :client nil))
         ;; and what that client said it can do ([[served-client-can?]], ADR-562)
         :served-client-can (fn () (table/get state :client-can #{}))}
        ;; the oldest message of any kind, tagged: the client's input as above, anything
        ;; else async app input — the event bus, as local displays deliver it — but not the
        ;; USER's, so it holds no idle timer back
        (display-polls (fn (ms)
                         (serve-busy-kick-tagged state (remote-receive-tagged state resume ms))))))))

(defn- serve-to (state deliver)
  "Call `deliver` with the client the display `state` serves now — or not at all while the
session is parked, with nobody to send to."
  (let (client (table/get state :client nil))
    (when client (deliver client))))

(defn- serve-seam (state resume kind id message)
  "Send a texture or sound `message` about `id` to the client — and, for a resuming session,
keep what is LIVE under `kind` (`:textures` / `:sounds`): the data message while it lives,
nothing once freed, so an adopting client is given exactly what the old one held."
  (do
    (when resume
      (let (live (table/get state kind {})
            freed? (contains? #{:texture-free :sound-free} (first message)))
        (table/put state kind (if freed? (dissoc live id) (assoc live id message)))))
    (serve-to state (fn (to) (send to message)))))

(defn- serve-send-frame (client frame)
  "Ship `frame` to `client` as `[:frame frame]`, superseding one still queued for it
(`proc/send-latest`, across the node link too — ADR-492): a daemon that renders faster
than the link or the client drains it queues one frame, not a backlog. A frame with a
one-shot op (`editor/display/frame-one-shots`: a sound, a burst) goes as a plain send,
which nothing supersedes — the sound plays even if a newer frame lands before the client
looks. Superseding moves the newest frame behind every texture and sound message sent
since the one it replaced, which is the order the daemon sent them in relative to it."
  (if (empty? (editor/display/frame-one-shots frame))
    (proc/send-latest client :editor/serve/frame [:frame frame])
    (send client [:frame frame])))

;; ---- the busy cursor across the link (ADR-491) ---------------------------------
;; A window shows the wait cursor while the loop that takes its input has been in one turn
;; past its quit key's busy-ms. A thin client's window feeds a loop on the DAEMON, which it
;; cannot ask, so the session asks for it: a watcher process beside the loop, kicked by each
;; input the loop takes, asks `proc/busy?` every `serve-busy-probe-ms` until the loop is idle
;; again, and tells the client each change as `[:busy on]`, newest-wins. As in the window,
;; nothing is asked at rest.

;; How often the watcher asks whether the loop is busy, from an input until it is idle —
;; the window's own `BUSY_PROBE`.
(def- serve-busy-probe-ms 100)

(defn- serve-send-busy (client on)
  "Tell `client` whether its session's loop is busy (`[:busy on]`), superseding a busy word
still queued for it (`proc/send-latest`): only the newest matters."
  (proc/send-latest client :editor/serve/busy [:busy on]))

(defn- serve-busy-start (state notify)
  "Start the calling loop's busy watcher, telling `notify` each change, and keep it in
`state` (under `:busy-watcher`) for the display's polls to kick. Runs in the loop process —
a display's `:enter` — so the watcher watches the loop. Returns nil."
  (let (loop (self))
    (table/put state :busy-watcher (spawn (serve-busy-watch loop notify))))
  nil)

(defn- serve-busy-kick (state input)
  "`input`, unchanged — and, when it is one (not nil), the busy watcher in `state` told the
loop may now be busy with it. Newest-wins, so a burst of keys queues one kick."
  (when (not (nil? input))
    (let (watcher (table/get state :busy-watcher nil))
      (when watcher (proc/send-latest watcher :editor/serve/kick :kick))))
  input)

(defn- serve-busy-kick-tagged (state tagged)
  "A `[message input?]` poll result, unchanged — kicking the busy watcher in `state` when
the message is the user's input (`serve-busy-kick`)."
  (when (and (not (nil? tagged)) (second tagged)) (serve-busy-kick state (first tagged)))
  tagged)

(defn- serve-busy-watch (loop notify)
  "The busy watcher of `loop`: monitor it, and end when it does."
  (monitor loop)
  (serve-busy-wait loop notify false))

(defn- serve-busy-wait (loop notify shown)
  "Idle: wait for a kick (the loop took an input) or the loop's end. `shown`: what `notify`
was last told."
  (receive
    (:kick (serve-busy-probe loop notify shown))
    ([:down _ ^loop _] nil)))

(defn- serve-busy-probe (loop notify shown)
  "Ask `loop` whether it is busy every `serve-busy-probe-ms`, telling `notify` each change,
until it is idle (no interrupt window open) — then wait for the next kick."
  (receive
    ([:down _ ^loop _] nil)
    (after serve-busy-probe-ms
      (let (busy (proc/busy? loop)
            show (= busy true))
        (when (not (= show shown)) (notify show))
        (if (nil? busy)
          (serve-busy-wait loop notify show)
          (serve-busy-probe loop notify show))))))

(defn- remote-receive-tagged (state resume ms)
  "A remote display's `:poll-tagged`: the oldest message, waiting up to `ms`, as
`[message input?]` — the client's keys, detach and death are input, and so are a manager's
hand-overs — or nil on timeout. A parked session waits no longer than its time to live,
and ends when that is up."
  (let (client (table/get state :client nil))
    (receive
      ([:key k held? number] (do
                               (remote-note-seq state number)
                               (table/put state :held? held?)
                               [(remote-note-resize state k) true]))
      ([:key k held?] (do (table/put state :held? held?) [(remote-note-resize state k) true]))
      ([:key k] [(remote-note-resize state k) true])
      ([:detach] [:close true])
      ((and message [:client-can _ _]) (remote-hand-over state resume message))
      ([:down _ ^client _] [(remote-client-lost state resume) true])
      ((and message [:adopt _ _ _ _]) [(remote-hand-over state resume message) true])
      ((and message [:claim _ _ _ _]) [(remote-hand-over state resume message) true])
      ;; a restarted loop has folded what was queued ahead of its client's input: take it
      ;; from now on (nil: a tick)
      ([:editor/serve/started] (do (table/put state :awaiting-start false) nil))
      (m [m false])
      (after (remote-parked-wait state resume ms) (remote-parked-expiry state resume)))))

(defn- remote-note-seq (state number)
  "Note in the session's ledger (`:ledger` in the display `state`) that the client's key
numbered `number` was taken — what a restarted loop tells the client it need not send again
(ADR-593). Returns nil."
  (let (ledger (table/get state :ledger nil))
    (when (and ledger (int? number)) (table/put ledger :taken number)))
  nil)

(defn- remote-note-resize (state input)
  "`input`, a key the client shipped — and when it is the client's `[:resize cols rows]` (a
terminal's SIGWINCH, a window dragged bigger), that size made the display's `:size` first,
so the session's next frame is laid out for the client as it is now: live resize, where a
client used to keep the size it attached with (ADR-587)."
  (do
    (when (and (vector? input)
            (= (count input) 3)
            (= (first input) :resize)
            (int? (nth input 1))
            (int? (nth input 2)))
      (table/put state :size [(nth input 1) (nth input 2)])
      (serve-report state))
    input))

(defn- serve-session-of (resume)
  "The session the manager knows this loop by: its supervisor (`:session` in `resume`,
ADR-593), or the loop itself for a display made without one."
  (or (:session resume) (self)))

(defn- serve-report (state)
  "Tell the session's supervisor (`:session` in the display `state`, when it has one) the
client this loop serves now, its size and when the session parked (nil while it has a
client), `[:editor/serve/session-state {:client :size :parked-at}]` — what a loop restarted
after this one died starts from (ADR-593). Returns nil."
  (let (session (table/get state :session nil))
    (when session
      (send session
        [:editor/serve/session-state
          {:client (table/get state :client nil)
           :size (table/get state :size nil)
           :parked-at (table/get state :parked-at nil)}])))
  nil)

(defn- serve-handled (state message)
  "Tell the session's supervisor (`:session` in the display `state`) that this loop handled
the manager's `message` — an `[:adopt …]`, a `[:claim …]`, a `[:client-can …]` — whatever
came of it, so a loop restarted after this one is not given it again (ADR-605): a hand-over
replayed to a loop whose session has since parked, or been taken over, would hand the
session to a client the manager gave another. Sent after what handling it sent, so the
supervisor folds it before this loop's death. Returns nil."
  (let (session (table/get state :session nil))
    (when session (send session [:editor/serve/handled message])))
  nil)

(defn- serve-ledger-clear (state)
  "Clear the session's key ledger (`:ledger` in the display `state`): a new client numbers
its keys from 1, so the last key the OLD client had taken says nothing about it — a restart
before the new client's first key was taken must send it again every key it kept (ADR-605).
Returns nil."
  (let (ledger (table/get state :ledger nil))
    (when ledger (table/delete ledger :taken)))
  nil)

(defn- serve-tell-attached (state client)
  "Tell `client` the loop it ships its input to — this one, `[:attached loop]` — and, for a
supervised session, the session itself (`[:session supervisor]`), whose end and not the
loop's ends the client: a loop that dies is restarted, and the restarted one says
`[:attached …]` again (ADR-593)."
  (send client [:attached (self)])
  (let (session (table/get state :session nil))
    (when session (send client [:session session])))
  nil)

(defn- remote-take-input (state resume)
  "The next input the client shipped this session — a key (noting whether one is held in
`state`), `:close` for a detach, its death ([[remote-client-lost]]), a new client handed
this session ([[remote-adopt]], [[remote-claim]]) — without waiting; nil when none came.
Only THE CLIENT's death counts: the session process may monitor other things (a collab
app monitors every hosted buffer process, whose `[:down …]` must reach its update as app
input for rehost/reshare, not tear the session down)."
  (let (client (table/get state :client nil))
    (receive
      ([:key k held? number] (do
                               (remote-note-seq state number)
                               (table/put state :held? held?)
                               (remote-note-resize state k)))
      ([:key k held?] (do (table/put state :held? held?) (remote-note-resize state k)))
      ; a client that does not say
      ([:key k] (remote-note-resize state k))
      ([:detach] :close)
      ;; what this client can be asked beyond keys and frames ([[served-client-can?]])
      ((and message [:client-can _ _]) (remote-hand-over state resume message))
      ; client process died / link net-split
      ([:down _ ^client _] (remote-client-lost state resume))
      ((and message [:adopt _ _ _ _]) (remote-hand-over state resume message))
      ((and message [:claim _ _ _ _]) (remote-hand-over state resume message))
      (after 0 nil))))

(defn- remote-hand-over (state resume message)
  "Handle the manager's `message` — `[:adopt …]` ([[remote-adopt]]), `[:claim …]`
([[remote-claim]]) or `[:client-can …]` ([[remote-note-capabilities]]) — and tell the
session it was handled ([[serve-handled]]), so no restarted loop is given it again. Answers
what handling it answered: the app's input, or nil."
  (let (input (match message
                ([:adopt token adopter cols rows] (remote-adopt state
                                                    resume token adopter cols rows))
                ([:claim token claimant cols rows] (remote-claim state
                                                     resume token claimant cols rows))
                ([:client-can from capabilities] (remote-note-capabilities state from capabilities))))
    (do (serve-handled state message) input)))

(defn- remote-note-capabilities (state from capabilities)
  "Note what the client `from` can do beyond keys and frames (`[:client-can from capabilities]`,
ADR-562: the session's own note of its first client's identity, the manager's on a hand-over)
— only for the client the session serves now, a stale one's word
counting for nothing. Nil: nothing for the loop to fold."
  (do
    (when (and (= from (table/get state :client nil))
            (or (vector? capabilities) (list? capabilities)))
      (table/put state :client-can (into #{} capabilities)))
    nil))

(defn- remote-client-lost (state resume)
  "The client died or its link dropped: `:close`, ending the session — or, for a session
that resumes, PARK it (nobody to draw to, the manager told) and hand the app
`[:client-lost]`."
  (if (nil? resume)
    :close (do
      (table/put state :client nil)
      (table/put state :client-can #{})
      (table/put state :monitor nil)
      (table/put state :held? false)
      (table/put state :parked-at (os/now))
      (serve-report state)
      (send (:manager resume) [:parked (serve-session-of resume) (:token resume)])
      [:client-lost])))

(defn- remote-adopt (state resume token client cols rows)
  "A new `client` (`cols`×`rows`) takes over this parked session: monitored, told the
session, given the window as it stands ([[serve-replay]]), its C-g armed — and the app is
handed `[:client-back]`. Only the manager's `token` adopts, and only a PARKED session: a
stray `[:adopt …]` must not take a live session's screen. Anything else is ignored (nil,
which the loop reads as a timeout)."
  (if (or (nil? resume) (not (= token (:token resume))) (not (nil? (table/get state :client nil))))
    nil
    (let (key (table/get state :quit-key nil))
      (do
        (table/put state :monitor (monitor client))
        (table/put state :client client)
        ;; what the new client can do the manager says next (`[:client-can …]`)
        (table/put state :client-can #{})
        (table/put state :size [cols rows])
        (table/put state :parked-at nil)
        ;; the new client numbers its keys from 1 (ADR-605)
        (serve-ledger-clear state)
        (serve-report state)
        (serve-tell-attached state client)
        (serve-replay state client)
        (when key (proc/set-quit-key [:key key] editor/ui/*ui-quit-busy-ms* client))
        [:client-back]))))

;; How long a session asked to hand itself over waits for its present client to say it is
;; still there. A live client answers in a round trip; one whose link dropped unnoticed
;; never does, and the user attaching again is waiting on this.
(def- serve-claim-ms 1500)

(defn- remote-claim (state resume token claimant cols rows)
  "The manager asks this session — which still has a client — whether `claimant`, attached
under the same identity, may take it over. It may when that client is gone without its
death being known yet: a link that dropped without closing, the laptop that slept, which
the user attaching again notices long before the network does. The client is asked
(`[:ping session ref]`, which every client loop answers `[:pong ref]`) and given
`serve-claim-ms`: an answer means two windows on purpose, and the manager is told to give
`claimant` a session of its own (`[:declined session claimant]`); silence or its death
hands this session to `claimant` ([[remote-adopt]]), the old client dropped and told
`[:bye]` in case its link comes back. A session parked meanwhile is simply adopted. Ignored
without the manager's token."
  (cond
    (or (nil? resume) (not (= token (:token resume)))) nil
    (nil? (table/get state :client nil))
      (do
        (send (:manager resume) [:claimed (serve-session-of resume) claimant])
        (remote-adopt state resume token claimant cols rows))
    else
      (let (old (table/get state :client nil)
            asked (ref))
        (do
          (send old [:ping (self) asked])
          (if (receive ([:pong ^asked] true) ([:down _ ^old _] false) (after serve-claim-ms false))
            (do (send (:manager resume) [:declined (serve-session-of resume) claimant]) nil)
            (let (watch (table/get state :monitor nil))
              (do
                (when watch (demonitor watch))
                (send old [:bye])
                (table/put state :client nil)
                (table/put state :client-can #{})
                (table/put state :monitor nil)
                (send (:manager resume) [:claimed (serve-session-of resume) claimant])
                (remote-adopt state resume token claimant cols rows))))))))

(defn- serve-replay (state client)
  "Give a `client` that took this session over the window as it stands: the window options
asked for so far, the textures and sound data still live, then the last frame — without
its sounds and bursts, which played when it was first drawn. A new grid redraws anyway;
the same grid would draw nothing until the model changed (ADR-444)."
  (let (options (table/get state :window nil)
        frame (table/get state :frame nil))
    (do
      (when options (send client [:window options]))
      (doseq (message (vals (table/get state :textures {}))) (send client message))
      (doseq (message (vals (table/get state :sounds {}))) (send client message))
      (when frame (send client [:frame (serve-without-one-shots frame)])))))

(def- serve-one-shot-tags #{:sound :burst})

(defn- serve-without-one-shots (ops)
  "`ops` (a frame, or a region's ops) without its one-shot ops — `[:sound …]`, `[:burst …]`
— at any depth: the frame as a screen, the inverse of `editor/display/frame-one-shots`."
  (if (vector? ops)
    (into []
      (map (seq/reject ops (fn (op) (and (vector? op) (contains? serve-one-shot-tags (first op)))))
        serve-without-one-shots))
    ops))

(defn- remote-parked-wait (state resume ms)
  "How long a poll may park: `ms`, or no longer than a parked session has left to live."
  (let (parked-at (table/get state :parked-at nil)
        lives (:ms resume))
    (if (or (nil? parked-at) (not (number? lives)))
      ms
      (math/max 0 (math/min ms (- (+ parked-at lives) (os/now)))))))

(defn- remote-parked-expiry (state resume)
  "A poll timed out: nil (the loop's tick) — or, for a session parked past its time to live,
the end of it: no client came back for it. The exit lands at the receive after it."
  (let (parked-at (table/get state :parked-at nil)
        lives (:ms resume))
    (if (and parked-at (number? lives) (>= (os/now) (+ parked-at lives)))
      (do (exit (self) :client-gone) (receive (after 0 nil)))
      nil)))
;; ---- daemon side -------------------------------------------------------------

(defn- serve-identity (client opts)
  "Who `client` is, as its session sees it: the identity `opts` map it attached with —
except that a client on a GUEST link (`node/grant-guest`, ADR-415) is marked `:guest true`
(the app decides what a guest may do) and, when its grant names who it is, gets THAT name
whatever its `opts` claim: the host chose it when it handed out the cookie, and the
client cannot."
  (let (grant (node/peer-grant client)
        claimed (if (map? opts) opts {}))
    (cond
      (nil? grant) claimed
      (string? (:as grant)) (assoc claimed :name (:as grant) :guest true)
      else (assoc claimed :guest true))))

;; ---- the session: a supervisor over its loop (ADR-593) ---------------------------
;; A session is TWO processes. The SESSION — the pid the manager knows, monitors and kills
;; — supervises; the LOOP, linked to it, runs the app's `ui-run` and is the pid the client
;; ships its input to. The session outlives its loop: when the loop dies abnormally, the
;; app's crash hook runs (`:on-crash` — an editor writes its unsaved buffers' sidecars
;; there) and, within the restart budget, a fresh loop is started for the same client from
;; what the dead one RETAINED ([[serve-retain]]) — an editor's buffer registry, whose
;; processes outlived the loop. The client is told the new loop (`[:reattached loop …]`),
;; sends it again every key the dead one never took, and keeps its window; it monitors the
;; session, not the loop, so only the session's end ends it.

;; How many times a session's loop may be restarted inside `serve-restart-window-ms` before
;; the session is let go — so a fault that recurs on every start cannot spin. The app's
;; `:supervise` map may say otherwise (`:max`, `:window-ms`).
(def- serve-restart-max 3)
(def- serve-restart-window-ms 60000)

;; The manager's hand-overs a session keeps for a loop restarted before it HANDLED them
;; (ADR-605): a loop says each one it handled ([[serve-handled]]) and the session forgets
;; it, so only the ones no loop took are given to a restarted one. Replaying a handled one
;; is NOT harmless — a claim replayed to a loop whose session has parked since adopts the
;; claimant without asking anyone, and one replayed to a loop serving the claimant asks the
;; claimant whether it is there. A bound on what a dead loop's backlog can hold, never reached
;; by one restart's gap.
(def- serve-pending-max 16)

;; The ways a loop ends WITHOUT failing: its app quit, its parked session's time ran out.
(def- serve-loop-ends #{:normal :client-gone})

(defn- serve-recent-deaths (deaths now window-ms)
  "The times among a session's loop `deaths` inside the last `window-ms` before `now` — all
the restart budget counts ([[serve-restart-allowed?]]), so all a session keeps."
  (into [] (seq/filter deaths (fn (at) (< (- now at) window-ms)))))

(defn- serve-restart-allowed? (deaths now max window-ms)
  "Whether a loop that died at `now` may be started again, given the times of the session's
earlier loop `deaths`: fewer than `max` of them inside the last `window-ms`."
  (< (count (serve-recent-deaths deaths now window-ms)) max))

(defn- serve-pending-handled (pending message)
  "The hand-overs `pending` without the first that equals `message`, which a loop handled."
  (let (at (index-of pending message))
    (if (< at 0) pending (into (into [] (take pending at)) (drop pending (inc at))))))

(defn- serve-session (client cols rows identity resume app)
  "One attached client's SESSION (ADR-593): this process supervises the app's loop
([[serve-loop]]), which it starts linked, and outlives it. `resume` (ADR-519) gains
`:session`, this pid, which the loop names itself by to the manager. Trapping exits, so the
loop's death arrives as a message ([[serve-supervise]]) and a `:kill` of this process —
`serve-stop` — takes the loop with it."
  (do
    (proc/trap-exit true)
    (serve-supervise
      (serve-start-loop
        {:client client
         :size [cols rows]
         :identity identity
         :capabilities {client (identity-capabilities identity)}
         :resume (when resume (assoc resume :session (self)))
         :app app
         :retained nil
         ;; when the session parked (nil while it has a client), kept across a restart
         :parked-at nil
         ;; the times of its loops' deaths inside the restart window, and how many restarts
         :deaths []
         :restarts 0
         ;; the manager's hand-overs forwarded to a loop that has not said it handled them
         :pending []
         ;; the last numbered key a loop took ([[remote-display]])
         :ledger (table/new)}
        (:make-model app)
        nil))))

(defn- serve-start-loop (session make-model death)
  "`session` with a fresh loop running ([[serve-loop]]), linked to this process: its model
from `(make-model)`, `death` what ended the loop before it (nil for the first)."
  (let (supervisor (self)
        loop-process (spawn-link (serve-loop supervisor session make-model death)))
    (assoc session :loop loop-process)))

(defn- serve-loop (supervisor session make-model death)
  "A session's LOOP: monitor the client (so its death tears the session down, or parks it
— see `remote-display`), tell it this loop and the `supervisor` session, then run the app's
`ui-run` against a `remote-display` to it. A fresh `(make-model)` per session keeps clients
independent. Queued to this loop's own mailbox before `ui-run` starts, so they are the first
events the app's `update` folds (through `remote-display`'s async pass-through): what the
client said it can do (ADR-562), a non-empty identity ([[serve-identity]]) as
`[:client-opts identity]` — the seam a collaborative app reads a participant's name from,
and a guest is known by — and, for a loop restarted after one died, `[:session-restarted
death]` (`{:reason :saved :restarting? :restarts :identity}`), its cue to say so. A
restarted loop tells its client only after those, in one `[:reattached loop session
last-taken]`: the client switches to it and sends again, in order, every key numbered after
`last-taken` — the keys the dead loop never took, typed into it or into the gap — which the
loop takes once its start events are folded. A loop of a parked session (no client) starts
parked."
  (let (client (:client session)
        [cols rows] (:size session)
        resume (:resume session)
        watch (when client (monitor client))
        ledger (:ledger session)
        display (remote-display client
                  cols
                  rows
                  (when resume (assoc resume :monitor watch))
                  {:session supervisor
                   :ledger ledger
                   :restarted? (not (nil? death))
                   :parked-at (:parked-at session)})
        identity (:identity session)
        app (:app session))
    (do
      ;; the first loop tells its client at once, as before supervision
      (when (and client (nil? death))
        (send client [:attached (self)])
        (send client [:session supervisor]))
      (when client (send (self) [:client-can client (get (:capabilities session) client [])]))
      (when (and (map? identity) (not (empty? identity)))
        (send (self) [:client-opts identity]))
      (when death
        (send (self) [:session-restarted death])
        ;; the end of what folds ahead of the client's input ([[remote-display]])
        (send (self) [:editor/serve/started])
        ;; and only now the client, in ONE message: this loop, the session, and the last key
        ;; the dead loop took — it sends every key after that again, before any new one
        (when client
          (send client [:reattached (self) supervisor (table/get ledger :taken nil)])))
      ;; bound for the loop's life, `make-model` included: who the session draws to now
      ;; ([[served-client]]) and the session itself ([[served-session]])
      (binding (editor/serve/*served-client* (get display :served-client)
                editor/serve/*served-client-can* (get display :served-client-can)
                editor/serve/*served-session* supervisor)
        (ui-run (make-model) (:view app) (:update app) display)))))

(defn- serve-supervise (session)
  "The session's loop over its running loop: keep what the loop retains and reports (its
client, size and when it parked), pass the manager's hand-overs on to it — keeping each until
the loop says it handled it, for a loop restarted before it did (ADR-605) — and when it
ends, [[serve-loop-ended]]."
  (let (loop-process (:loop session))
    (receive
      ([:editor/serve/retain value] (serve-supervise (assoc session :retained value)))
      ([:editor/serve/session-state facts] (serve-supervise (assoc session
                                                              :client (:client facts)
                                                              :size
                                                                (or (:size facts) (:size session))
                                                              :parked-at (:parked-at facts))))
      ;; a loop handled one: no restarted loop is given it again
      ([:editor/serve/handled message] (serve-supervise (assoc session
                                                          :pending
                                                            (serve-pending-handled (:pending session)
                                                              message))))
      ;; the manager's word for the loop: a parked session adopted, one asked to hand itself
      ;; over, what a client can do
      ((and message (or [:adopt _ _ _ _] [:claim _ _ _ _] [:client-can _ _])) (do
                                                                                (send loop-process
                                                                                  message)
                                                                                (serve-supervise (serve-note-forwarded session
                                                                                                   message))))
      ([:EXIT ^loop-process reason] (serve-loop-ended session reason))
      (_ (serve-supervise session)))))

(defn- serve-note-forwarded (session message)
  "`session` after passing the manager's `message` to its loop: kept until the loop says it
handled it (at most `serve-pending-max`), and a client's capabilities noted (`[:client-can
client capabilities]`), so a restarted loop knows what its client can do."
  (let (pending (conj (:pending session) message)
        kept (if (> (count pending) serve-pending-max) (into [] (rest pending)) pending))
    (match message
      ([:client-can client capabilities] (assoc session
                                           :pending kept
                                           :capabilities
                                             (assoc (:capabilities session)
                                               client capabilities)))
      (_ (assoc session :pending kept)))))

(defn- serve-crash-hook (on-crash retained death)
  "Run the app's crash hook `(on-crash retained death)` and answer what it returns — nil
when it fails, which is said on stderr: a hook that cannot save must not also cost the
restart."
  (check-allow :discarded-catch
    (try
      (on-crash retained death)
      (catch e
        (do
          (io/puts (str "serve: the session's crash hook failed: " (error-message e)) :to *err*)
          nil)))))

(defn- serve-loop-ended (session reason)
  "The session's loop ended with `reason`. A quit or a parked session's expiry
(`serve-loop-ends`) ends the session. Any other end is a death: the app's crash hook runs
(`:on-crash` in its `:supervise` options — [[serve-crash-hook]]) with what the loop retained
and `{:reason :restarting?}`, and then, when the app can restart one (`:restart`) and the
budget allows ([[serve-restart-allowed?]]), a fresh loop starts over the retained value for
the same client (or parked, when it had none, since the session parked), given again the
manager's hand-overs no loop handled (ADR-605); otherwise the session ends, and its client
with it. The session keeps only the deaths the budget still counts. The session ends with
its loop's reason ([[serve-end]])."
  (if (contains? serve-loop-ends reason)
    (serve-end reason)
    (let (now (os/now)
          supervise (or (:supervise (or (:opts (:app session)) {})) {})
          restart (:restart supervise)
          window-ms (or (:window-ms supervise) serve-restart-window-ms)
          deaths (serve-recent-deaths (:deaths session) now window-ms)
          restarting? (and (fn? restart)
                        (serve-restart-allowed? deaths
                          now
                          (or (:max supervise) serve-restart-max)
                          window-ms))
          on-crash (:on-crash supervise)
          retained (:retained session)
          saved (when (fn? on-crash)
                  (serve-crash-hook on-crash retained {:reason reason :restarting? restarting?}))
          restarts (inc (or (:restarts session) 0))
          death {:reason reason
                 :saved saved
                 :restarting? restarting?
                 :restarts restarts
                 :identity (:identity session)})
      (if restarting?
        (let (client (:client session)
              restarted (serve-start-loop (assoc session
                                            :deaths (conj deaths now)
                                            :restarts restarts)
                          (fn () (restart retained death))
                          death))
          (do
            ;; the dead loop may have died busy: its client's wait cursor goes
            (when client (serve-send-busy client false))
            (doseq (message (:pending session)) (send (:loop restarted) message))
            (serve-supervise restarted)))
        (serve-end reason)))))

(defn- serve-end (reason)
  "End the session with its loop's `reason`, so whoever watches the session (its client,
the manager) learns how it ended as a watcher of the loop did: return for `:normal`, else
exit with it — no longer trapping, so the exit is this process's own and not a message."
  (when (not (= reason :normal))
    (proc/trap-exit false)
    (exit (self) reason)
    (receive (after 0 nil)))
  reason)

(defn served-session ()
  "The SESSION the served loop the caller runs in belongs to (ADR-593) — its supervisor's
pid, which outlives the loop: a loop that dies is restarted under it. What should live as
long as the session and no longer is tied to it (a buffer registry's `{:owner …}`), and what
a restarted loop starts from is handed to it ([[serve-retain]]). Nil outside a `serve`
session.

    (editor/serve/served-session)   ;=> nil"
  editor/serve/*served-session*)

(defn serve-retain (value)
  "Hand the session the served loop the caller runs in belongs to ([[served-session]])
`value`: what its app's `:supervise` hooks are given if this loop dies — `:on-crash` to
save from, `:restart` to build the next loop's model from (ADR-593). The latest value
retained wins; plain data and pids (an editor's buffer registry, the flags its model
carries), since it crosses into the session's process. Returns `value`; nil, keeping
nothing, outside a served session.

    (editor/serve/serve-retain {:registry nil})   ;=> nil"
  (let (session (served-session))
    (if (nil? session) nil (do (send session [:editor/serve/retain value]) value))))

;; ---- the client's clipboard, across the link (ADR-539) --------------------------
;; A served app runs on the daemon, but the clipboard its user means is the one on the
;; machine whose window they are looking at: the CLIENT's. So a session does not touch the
;; daemon's OS clipboard; it asks its client. Two messages, both data:
;;
;;   session -> client  [:clipboard-set text]          put `text` on the client's clipboard
;;   session -> client  [:clipboard-get session ref]   what is on it?
;;   client -> session  [:clipboard ref text]           the answer (`text` nil when empty)
;;
;; Nothing waits. A set is a cast; a get is ASKED (`client-clipboard-ask`) and its answer
;; reaches the app's `update` as an event, like any other background message — so the
;; session's loop never stops for the round trip. A client that is gone without its death
;; being known yet (ADR-519's dropped link) answers nothing, so the ask also schedules its
;; own answer, `[:clipboard ref nil]`, `serve-clipboard-ms` later: the app hears about every
;; ask exactly once if it takes the first answer for a ref and ignores the rest.

;; How long a session gives its client to say what is on its clipboard before answering
;; itself nil. A live client answers in a round trip.
(def- serve-clipboard-ms 500)

(defn served-client ()
  "The client (its pid) the served session the caller runs in draws to now — nil when the
caller is not a `serve` session's process, or the session is parked with no client
(ADR-519). What a served app asks of the machine its user sits at goes there.

    (editor/serve/served-client)   ;=> nil"
  (let (client-of editor/serve/*served-client*)
    (if (nil? client-of) nil (client-of))))

(defn client-clipboard-set (text)
  "Put `text` on the clipboard of the machine this served session's client runs on — the
user's, not the daemon's (ADR-539) — and return `text`; nil, sending nothing, outside a
served session or while it is parked. A cast: nothing waits for the client.

    (editor/serve/client-clipboard-set \"hello\")   ;=> nil"
  (let (client (served-client))
    (if (nil? client) nil (do (send client [:clipboard-set text]) text))))

(defn client-clipboard-ask (&optional (ms serve-clipboard-ms))
  "Ask the machine this served session's client runs on what is on its clipboard (ADR-539),
without waiting: the answer arrives in the session's mailbox — and so at the app's `update`
— as `[:clipboard ref text]`, `text` nil when the clipboard is empty or not text. Returns
`ref`, to match the answer by; nil, asking nothing, outside a served session and while it is
parked. A client that does not answer within `ms` (500 by default) is answered for:
`[:clipboard ref nil]` arrives then, so the app hears about every ask. Its real answer may
still come after that; take the first `[:clipboard ref …]` for a ref and ignore the rest.

    (editor/serve/client-clipboard-ask)   ;=> nil"
  (let (client (served-client))
    (if (nil? client)
      nil
      (let (asked (ref))
        (do
          (send client [:clipboard-get (self) asked])
          (timer/send-after ms (self) [:clipboard asked nil])
          asked)))))

;; The PRIMARY selection (ADR-553) crosses the link the same way (ADR-562): what the user
;; highlights in a served app is the CLIENT machine's PRIMARY, and a middle click pastes the
;; client's — three more messages, the clipboard's shape with its own tags:
;;
;;   session -> client  [:primary-set text]          own the client's PRIMARY with `text`
;;   session -> client  [:primary-get session ref]   what is in it?
;;   client -> session  [:primary ref text]           the answer (`text` nil when empty)
;;
;; Sent only to a client that SAID it handles them (`:can [:primary]` in its attach identity,
;; [[served-client-can?]]): an older client's window ships an unknown message
;; back as a key, and its terminal lets them pile up, so a session serving one has no PRIMARY.

(defn served-client-can? (capability)
  "Whether the client the served session the caller runs in draws to now said it can do
`capability` (`:primary`, ADR-562) — false outside a served session, while it is parked, and
for a client that said nothing (one older than the saying).

    (editor/serve/served-client-can? :primary)   ;=> false"
  (let (can-of editor/serve/*served-client-can*)
    (and (not (nil? (served-client))) (not (nil? can-of)) (contains? (can-of) capability))))

(defn client-primary-set (text)
  "Own the PRIMARY selection of the machine this served session's client runs on with `text`
(ADR-562) — what a drag highlights there, pasteable by a middle click in the user's other
apps — and return `text`; nil, sending nothing, outside a served session, while it is parked,
or when its client cannot ([[served-client-can?]]). A cast, as [[client-clipboard-set]].

    (editor/serve/client-primary-set \"hello\")   ;=> nil"
  (if (served-client-can? :primary)
    (do (send (served-client) [:primary-set text]) text)
    nil))

(defn client-primary-ask (&optional (ms serve-clipboard-ms))
  "Ask the machine this served session's client runs on what its PRIMARY selection holds
(ADR-562), without waiting: the answer reaches the app's `update` as `[:primary ref text]`,
`text` nil when it is empty, not text, or the machine has none. Returns `ref`; nil, asking
nothing, outside a served session, while it is parked, or when its client cannot
([[served-client-can?]]) — the app then has no PRIMARY. Answered for after `ms` as
[[client-clipboard-ask]] is: take the first `[:primary ref …]` for a ref.

    (editor/serve/client-primary-ask)   ;=> nil"
  (if (served-client-can? :primary)
    (let (asked (ref))
      (do
        (send (served-client) [:primary-get (self) asked])
        (timer/send-after ms (self) [:primary asked nil])
        asked))
    nil))

(defn- serve-resumes? (opts identity)
  "Whether a session for `identity` parks when its client is lost: the server resumes
(`:resume-ms`) and the client said who it is — an anonymous client's session could be
handed to anyone."
  (and (map? opts) (not (nil? (:resume-ms opts))) (string? (:name identity))))

;; The manager's state, `live`: `:sessions` (pid -> `{:identity :token :parked?}`) and
;; `:pending` (a client waiting while sessions under its identity are asked to hand
;; themselves over: client -> `{:asking session :others [session …] :cols :rows
;; :identity}`). `app` is what a new session is made of: `{:make-model :view :update
;; :opts}`.

(defn- serve-forget (live kind key)
  "`live` without `key` in its `kind` table (`:sessions` or `:pending`). Not `update`:
inside `serve-manager` that name is the APP's update function, which a call meant for the
manager's own state once reached."
  (assoc live kind (dissoc (get live kind) key)))

(defn- serve-spawn (live app client cols rows identity)
  "`live` with a NEW session for `client` — resuming when the server does and `client` is
named."
  (let (opts (:opts app)
        token (ref)
        resume (when (serve-resumes? opts identity)
                 {:ms (:resume-ms opts) :manager (self) :token token})
        session (spawn (serve-session client cols rows identity resume app)))
    (do
      (monitor session)
      (map/assoc-in live [:sessions session] {:identity identity :token token :parked? false}))))

(defn- serve-ask (live app client cols rows identity candidates)
  "Ask the first of `candidates` still alive to hand itself over to `client`
([[remote-claim]]), keeping the rest for if it declines — or, with none left, give `client`
a session of its own."
  (let (alive (seq/filter candidates (fn (session) (contains? (:sessions live) session))))
    (if (empty? alive)
      (serve-spawn live app client cols rows identity)
      (let (session (first alive))
        (do
          (send session [:claim (map/get-in live [:sessions session :token]) client cols rows])
          ;; and what the claimant can do, which the session takes once it is the claimant's
          (send session [:client-can client (identity-capabilities identity)])
          (map/assoc-in live
            [:pending client]
            {:asking session :others (rest alive) :cols cols :rows rows :identity identity}))))))

(defn- serve-ask-next (live app client)
  "The session `client` waited on declined or died: ask the next ([[serve-ask]])."
  (let (entry (map/get-in live [:pending client]))
    (serve-ask (serve-forget live :pending client)
      app
      client
      (:cols entry)
      (:rows entry)
      (:identity entry)
      (:others entry))))

(defn- serve-attach (live app client cols rows given)
  "`live` after `client` attached with identity `given`: handed the PARKED session of the
same identity when the server resumes; else, when a session of that identity still has a
client, that session asked whether its client is really there ([[serve-ask]] — a dropped
link is often noticed by the user attaching again before the network notices it); else a
session of its own."
  (let (identity (serve-identity client given)
        mine (if (serve-resumes? (:opts app) identity)
               (seq/filter (keys (:sessions live))
                 ;; who, not what their client can do: a newer client resumes the same session
                 (fn (session)
                   (= (dissoc (map/get-in live [:sessions session :identity]) :can)
                     (dissoc identity :can))))
               [])
        parked (seq/find mine (fn (session) (map/get-in live [:sessions session :parked?]))))
    (cond
      parked
        (do
          (send parked [:adopt (map/get-in live [:sessions parked :token]) client cols rows])
          (send parked [:client-can client (identity-capabilities identity)])
          (map/assoc-in live [:sessions parked :parked?] false))
      (not (empty? mine)) (serve-ask live app client cols rows identity mine)
      else (serve-spawn live app client cols rows identity))))

(defn- serve-session-down (live app session)
  "`live` after `session` ended: forgotten, and every client waiting on its answer moved
on to the next ([[serve-ask-next]])."
  (let (gone (serve-forget live :sessions session)
        waiting (seq/filter (keys (:pending gone))
                  (fn (client) (= (map/get-in gone [:pending client :asking]) session))))
    (fold waiting gone (fn (acc client) (serve-ask-next acc app client)))))

(defn- serve-manager (make-model view update &optional opts live)
  "The session manager loop (registered under `serve-name`): on each `[:attach client
cols rows]` / `[:attach client cols rows opts]`, give that client a session
([[serve-attach]]: its own, the parked one it left, or the one whose old client turns out
to be gone — `opts` is the client's identity map, `{}` for an old-style attach). Live
sessions are tracked (monitored; pruned on `[:down …]`, marked on `[:parked …]`, answers
to a hand-over on `[:claimed …]` / `[:declined …]`) so `[:shutdown from r]` — the
`serve-stop` message — can end every one of them: each killed session's client sees its
monitor fire and closes cleanly. Tail-recursive."
  (let (state (or live {:sessions {} :pending {}})
        app {:make-model make-model :view view :update update :opts opts}
        again (fn (next) (serve-manager make-model view update opts next)))
    (receive
      ([:attach client cols rows] (again (serve-attach state app client cols rows {})))
      ([:attach client cols rows given] (again (serve-attach state app client cols rows given)))
      ([:parked session token] (again (if (= token (map/get-in state [:sessions session :token]))
                                        (map/assoc-in state [:sessions session :parked?] true)
                                        state)))
      ([:claimed _session client] (again (serve-forget state :pending client)))
      ([:declined session client] (again (if (= session
                                               (map/get-in state [:pending client :asking]))
                                           (serve-ask-next state app client)
                                           state)))
      ([:down _ p _] (again (serve-session-down state app p)))
      ([:shutdown from r] (let (sessions (keys (:sessions state)))
                            (do
                              (doseq (s sessions) (exit s :kill))
                              (send from [:reply r (count sessions)]))))
      (_ (again state)))))

(defn serve-stop (&optional mgr)
  "Stop serving: kill every live session (each client's monitor fires and its window
closes) and end the manager — `mgr`, or the one registered under `serve-name`.
Returns the number of sessions ended, nil when nothing was serving, `:no-reply` on a
wedged manager. The counterpart of `serve`.

    (editor/serve/serve-stop)   ;~> 2 (sessions ended), or nil when nothing was serving"
  (let (pid (or mgr (proc/whereis serve-name)))
    (if (nil? pid)
      nil
      (let (r (ref))
        (send pid [:shutdown (self) r])
        (receive ([:reply ^r v] v) (after 2000 :no-reply))))))

(defn serve (make-model view update &optional opts)
  "Serve a `ui-run` app from THIS runtime to attaching frontends (ADR-090). `make-model`
is a 0-arg thunk producing a *fresh* initial model per client; `view`/`update` are the
usual `ui-run` functions. Registers a session manager under `serve-name` and returns
its pid (non-blocking) — a daemon's `main` typically calls `(serve …)` then parks
(`nest run --name N` brings the node up). Each `(attach …)` / `nest attach` then gets
its own session. The program must already be a node (`node-start` / `nest run --name`).
Opt-in: a runtime serves nothing unless it calls `serve`.

`opts` `{:resume-ms N}` makes a session OUTLIVE its client (ADR-519): when a named client
(one that attached with `{:name …}`) dies or its link drops, its session parks for `N` ms
(or `:forever`) with all its state, and the next attach under the same identity takes it
over where it was — the emacsclient whose daemon kept its buffers through a dropped ssh.
An attach under that identity while its session still has a client asks the old client
whether it is there, and takes the session over when it is not: the link that dropped
without anyone noticing yet. The app folds `[:client-lost]` and `[:client-back]` as it
goes (see `remote-display`).

Every session SUPERVISES its loop (ADR-593): the session is a process of its own, which the
loop runs linked under and the client watches. `opts` `{:supervise {:on-crash f :restart g}}`
says what a loop that dies abnormally costs: `(f retained death)` runs first — the place to
save what the loop held (an editor's unsaved buffers, as auto-save sidecars) — and then
`(g retained death)` makes the model a fresh loop starts from, for the same client (parked,
when it had none), which is told the new loop and keeps its window; the app folds
`[:session-restarted death]` first. `retained` is what the loop last handed
[[serve-retain]] (nil if nothing), `death` `{:reason :restarting? …}` (`:saved`, the hook's
answer, `:restarts`, and the client's `:identity` for `g`, so a guest's session is still one
before its first key). At most `:max` restarts (3) inside `:window-ms` (60000):
past that, and without a `:restart`, the hook still runs and the session ends — its client
with it, as before supervision. A quit, or a parked session's expiry, is no death.

    (editor/serve/serve (fn () {:n 0}) view update)   ;~> #<pid …> of the session manager, registered as :ui
    (editor/serve/serve (fn () {:n 0}) view update {:resume-ms 3600000})   ;~> the same, a dropped client's session kept an hour"
  (proc/register serve-name (spawn (serve-manager make-model view update opts))))

;; ---- shared session (many frontends, ONE model — collaborative) --------------
;; `serve` gives each client its OWN model (independent sessions). `serve-shared` runs a
;; SINGLE `ui-run` and fans its frames to every attached client while merging all their
;; input into the one loop — so everyone edits the same buffers and sees each other's
;; edits live (the daemon-`make-frame`/collaborative model). The shape is `display-broadcast`
;; made dynamic: a **hub** process holds the changing client set + the latest frame, the
;; session's display `:draw`s to the hub (which fans out) and `:poll`s the merged key stream.
;;
;; This slice shares ONE cursor (the single model's point): every frontend shows the same
;; point. Distinct per-participant cursors (each user their own caret/selection) need
;; edit-surviving **markers** in `std/buffer` + point moved off the buffer onto a
;; participant — deferred (see the myedit `docs/remote-multiplayer-plan.md`, Slice 2c, and
;; `docs/actor-architecture.md`). A shared session keeps the size it started with: its
;; clients may each be another size, where a `serve` session has one client to follow.

(defn- shared-hub (clients frame size)
  "The fan-out hub for a shared session: the changing set of attached client pids, the
latest `frame`, and the session `size`. `[:add pid]` adds a client (seeding it with the
current frame, if any); `[:remove pid]` drops one; `[:frame f]` stores + broadcasts to
every client; `[:size reply]` replies `[:hub-size c r]`; `[:shutdown]` tells all clients to
close (`[:bye]`). Tail-recursive; one hub per shared session."
  (receive
    ([:add pid] (do
                  (when frame (send pid [:frame frame]))
                  (shared-hub (cons pid clients) frame size)))
    ([:remove pid] (shared-hub (seq/reject clients (fn (p) (= p pid))) frame size))
    ([:frame f] (do (doseq (p clients) (serve-send-frame p f)) (shared-hub clients f size)))
    ;; the shared loop is busy, or no longer: every client's window shows it (ADR-491)
    ([:busy on] (do (doseq (p clients) (serve-send-busy p on)) (shared-hub clients frame size)))
    ([:size reply] (do
                     (send reply [:hub-size (first size) (second size)])
                     (shared-hub clients frame size)))
    ([:shutdown] (doseq (p clients) (send p [:bye])))))

(defn- shared-hub-size (hub)
  "Query `hub` for the shared session's `[cols rows]` (a local round-trip), falling back to
`[80 24]` if it doesn't answer promptly. Selective on `[:hub-size …]`, so it never eats the
`[:key …]` inputs also queued on the session's mailbox."
  (send hub [:size (self)])
  (receive ([:hub-size c r] [c r]) (after 200 [80 24])))

(defn- shared-display (hub)
  "The `ui-run` display for a shared session: `:draw` ships each frame to `hub` (fanned to
every client); `:poll` returns the next `[:key k]` any client shipped (all clients' input
merges through the session's one mailbox, so edits serialize with no conflict); `:size`
asks the hub; `:leave` shuts the hub (and thus every client) down. As with
`remote-display`, any other mailbox message is returned as async app input (the event
bus) — except a stale `[:hub-size …]` reply that outlived its `shared-hub-size` window,
which is dropped (a nil ⇒ tick) rather than leaked to the app."
  (let (state (table/new))
    (merge
      {:enter (fn () (serve-busy-start state (fn (on) (send hub [:busy on]))))
       :leave (fn () (send hub [:shutdown]))
       :size (fn () (shared-hub-size hub))
       :draw (fn (f) (send hub [:frame f]))
       ;; any client's key, ahead of the session's background messages (ADR-490)
       :poll-input (fn () (serve-busy-kick state (shared-take-key)))
       :quit-message (fn (key) [:key key])}
      (display-polls (fn (ms) (serve-busy-kick-tagged state (shared-receive-tagged ms)))))))

(defn- shared-receive-tagged (ms)
  "A shared session's `:poll-tagged`: the oldest message, waiting up to `ms`, as
`[message input?]` — any client's key is input, anything else is async app input (the
event bus) — or nil on timeout, and for a `:size` reply past its 200ms window, which is
dropped rather than leaked to the app."
  (receive
    ([:key k _held? _seq] [k true])
    ([:key k _held?] [k true])
    ([:key k] [k true])
    ([:hub-size _ _] nil)
    (m [m false])
    (after ms nil)))

(defn- shared-take-key ()
  "The next key any client of a shared session shipped, without waiting; nil when none
came. Whether a key is down is per client, and one shared model has no client to ask, so
a shared session keeps its idle timers."
  (receive ([:key k _held? _seq] k) ([:key k _held?] k) ([:key k] k) (after 0 nil)))

(defn- serve-shared-manager (session hub)
  "The session manager for a SHARED session: every client attaches to the SAME running
`session`. On `[:attach client …]` (with or without an identity `opts` — one shared model
has no per-participant state, so opts are accepted and ignored), add the client to `hub`
and tell it the shared session pid (so it ships keys there); monitor it so its death drops
it from the hub. Tail-recursive."
  (receive
    ([:attach client _cols _rows] (do
                                    (monitor client)
                                    (send hub [:add client])
                                    (send client [:attached session])
                                    (serve-shared-manager session hub)))
    ([:attach client _cols _rows _opts] (do
                                          (monitor client)
                                          (send hub [:add client])
                                          (send client [:attached session])
                                          (serve-shared-manager session hub)))
    ([:down _ client _] (do (send hub [:remove client]) (serve-shared-manager session hub)))
    ;; serve-stop: tell every client goodbye (the hub fans [:bye]), end the session
    ([:shutdown from r] (do (send hub [:shutdown]) (exit session :kill) (send from [:reply r 1])))
    (_ (serve-shared-manager session hub))))

(defn serve-shared (model view update)
  "Serve a SINGLE shared `ui-run` session to many frontends — collaborative editing: all
clients attach to ONE `model` and see each other's edits live (contrast `serve`, a fresh
independent model per client). Spawns the hub, the shared session (`ui-run` over
`shared-display`), and the manager, and registers the manager under `serve-name`. Clients
attach with the same `attach` / `attach-display` (the client side is identical — it just
ships keys and paints frames; whether the daemon is per-client or shared is transparent).
All clients share ONE cursor this slice; see the module note for the per-participant-cursor
deferral. Returns the manager pid.

    (editor/serve/serve-shared {:text \"\"} view update)   ;~> #<pid …> of the manager, registered as :ui"
  (let (hub (spawn (shared-hub () nil [80 24]))
        session (spawn (ui-run model view update (shared-display hub))))
    (proc/register serve-name (spawn (serve-shared-manager session hub)))))
